db/ref 17592186409586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436629}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"} :step/id #uuid "f956015e-ca45-5f2f-8813-7e7f2a215cba" :step/math-academy-id 139690}] :activity/title "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059925}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409588} :activity/id #uuid "f39321ff-9db6-52db-bca8-a10eccf4ff9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805 :topic/id #uuid "155403ed-fbfa-59a9-b928-e762a73145ef" :topic/math-academy-id 247} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d0f8fb4-d1b2-5bff-ba27-c6e4e679e31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453634} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7d44c8-3ad6-50d2-9658-d18a60e921c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453635} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba7a927-c65d-53d2-a68e-2a711452828e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8c84692-3840-51b5-880d-3386d31c4f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453636} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc1d84f-0a08-51e1-9122-944928bd54c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453637} :answer-field/id #uuid "91aa8150-4bc8-561c-9c32-8cfddb99b464" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f8ce0ad-e15d-5071-aae6-6ebf6f10905b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453638} {:answer/id #uuid "34bd4c0a-6891-5927-8f43-1900cd830078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453638} :answer-field/id #uuid "728d40e4-2d8b-5727-be86-396f6263ab1f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1308109d-5efe-51ad-bfa1-4050522344dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-182506" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$\n- **II.** $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$\n- **III.** $¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7e76a699-413c-5c79-9a86-67728b6bb289"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191c421c-bbe9-5286-9beb-03027543bcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B∨¬C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409566} {:answer/id #uuid "33e9615f-fd80-5202-949f-ebfef4f3e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬B∨C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409569} {:answer/id #uuid "35dc661b-8042-5dd9-ae2c-1ddb0493316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B∧¬C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409570} {:answer/id #uuid "442d26c2-04f3-57d7-adf7-53465469c631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬B∧C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409572} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2b2d55-0e4e-5769-9706-03ec9ecc763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B∨C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409573}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409566} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea492f1-156d-5834-96c0-8454fc274282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7953127-b916-5d8d-98b4-eeece8a2e1ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-81892" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $(¬B∧C)⇒(B∨¬C).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n(¬B∧C)⇒(B∨¬C)≡¬(¬B∧C)∨(B∨¬C).\n$$\n\nWe can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(¬B∧C)∨(B∨¬C)≡(¬(¬B)∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n(¬(¬B)∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)≡(B∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C).\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the disjunction of a statement with itself:\n\n$$\n(B∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)≡B∨¬C\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $B∨¬C.$"} :step/id #uuid "b1214e01-49e9-573c-a4c8-4d572ee5620a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b57f374-7974-5938-b11d-cfd731887fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∨¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409567} {:answer/id #uuid "33e6f69b-ceb9-546e-bc81-3a178a362d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬Y∨X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409568} {:answer/id #uuid "42a65b2d-e5f8-5a76-b55d-46a8613eea7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬Y∧X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409571} {:answer/id #uuid "a433720c-898a-5e82-b529-c83bbf9df930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409577} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b85a9a-afc4-5057-b50b-a3fabfd591bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409568} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc790e07-5a28-514e-aa55-ed9af585fbba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b7c7494-65e2-5888-808a-8604d9b7cfa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-182492" :question/problem "$Y⇒X\\,≡$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nY⇒X\\,≡\\,¬Y∨X\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ede73126-39fd-544c-9a47-ac55caf10274" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409587}}] :activity/title "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409565}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422172} :activity/id #uuid "ad31faf4-6ba4-59e5-b6eb-0f068c9137a4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805 :topic/id #uuid "155403ed-fbfa-59a9-b928-e762a73145ef" :topic/math-academy-id 247} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d0148c-06ef-5117-bace-03063dd6360d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A∨B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378714} {:answer/id #uuid "f9df4014-2df2-5124-bf2b-638e928d7002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬B∨A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378715} {:answer/id #uuid "39a579bf-496b-548c-bd9f-6607183041f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬B∧A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378716} {:answer/id #uuid "c70b7f6e-bb66-5469-8638-e480696d51ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬A∧B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378717} {:answer/id #uuid "469bc761-0a98-5c2a-9f58-31c271c74501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A∨¬B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378714} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef1e854-dcf0-5bf2-bce1-3c3b1e51137e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe611b4a-33af-5b80-a6da-077998756db6" :question/math-academy-id "q-1469" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬A⇒B.$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬A⇒B≡¬(¬A)∨B\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬A)∨B≡A∨B.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $A∨B.$"} :step/id #uuid "12d4c7cd-cbe3-5cf6-98ac-5ef2b58306b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422174}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b57f374-7974-5938-b11d-cfd731887fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∨¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409567} {:answer/id #uuid "33e6f69b-ceb9-546e-bc81-3a178a362d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬Y∨X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409568} {:answer/id #uuid "42a65b2d-e5f8-5a76-b55d-46a8613eea7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬Y∧X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409571} {:answer/id #uuid "a433720c-898a-5e82-b529-c83bbf9df930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409577} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b85a9a-afc4-5057-b50b-a3fabfd591bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409568} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc790e07-5a28-514e-aa55-ed9af585fbba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b7c7494-65e2-5888-808a-8604d9b7cfa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-182492" :question/problem "$Y⇒X\\,≡$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nY⇒X\\,≡\\,¬Y∨X\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "992a12e5-4030-5d06-bfe4-99bff662d13d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422176}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422174 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911a45b8-ba13-5065-bb06-c450effaabf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394326} {:answer/id #uuid "95480722-7569-5083-8b58-56b83301d195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394327} {:answer/id #uuid "8476685e-e104-531e-86f2-c09e6663f600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394328} {:answer/id #uuid "eee8dbbe-fc4d-5c20-9c21-6ae1d839c1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394329} {:answer/id #uuid "77a3543d-dc9d-5f25-b6e9-2b5f550e3919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394329} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f0c2ff-63c9-5651-8370-33bd7ba743eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0467eb40-db5b-5041-af5a-7039ef1e5d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-81891" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(X∧Y)⇒Y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(X∧Y)⇒Y≡¬(¬(X∧Y))∨Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(X∧Y))∨Y≡(X∧Y)∨Y.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n(X∧Y)∨Y≡Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $Y.$"} :step/id #uuid "a4bf2a7e-915f-5aaa-9350-9113ac1d5a6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422175}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422175 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173a22f9-03eb-5f52-84a2-64add79bb270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P∧(Q∧R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422158} {:answer/id #uuid "2655898f-0ab3-55b9-b970-24883e6a1310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P∨(Q∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422159} {:answer/id #uuid "666720f8-ee5e-5a0c-9eff-9b28566b29c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P∧(Q∨¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422161} {:answer/id #uuid "be278a98-7931-527b-9249-5fe023ad7c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P∧(Q∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422166} {:answer/id #uuid "d2702799-977f-5026-b7cd-3d37fe168a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬P∧(Q∨R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422167}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422166} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19fca36-2b89-53ba-85fc-7765f2211e66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2bdcd5e4-a0e7-512a-97bb-f847c06388f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82451" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(P∧Q)⇒(P∧R).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(P∧Q)⇒(P∧R)≡¬(¬(P∧Q))∨(P∧R).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(P∧Q))∨(P∧R)≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧R).\n$$\n\nFinally, by the distributive laws, we have\n\n$$\n(P∧Q)∨(P∧R)≡P∧(Q∨R).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $P∧(Q∨R).$"} :step/id #uuid "b4e7f46a-d2bb-5a13-8fe3-d393ec767bb7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422176 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c9c24e-3b55-56ca-86a7-b2ce01e8f281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422160} {:answer/id #uuid "719cf9b3-0ac6-54a8-b955-880ab8843c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you apologize, then she will forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422162} {:answer/id #uuid "8213100f-0dc7-5d4d-b8fd-6e029e273dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you apologize, then she won't forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422163} {:answer/id #uuid "a2794c58-1f77-5658-9986-016411aec9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you don't apologize, then she will forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422164} {:answer/id #uuid "a2993367-1981-5e54-b3ef-99b9be03f44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Apologize, and she will forgive you." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422160} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83d217a-41be-555a-a16a-343371ec8880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c4482556-1279-5932-bd2e-cbe957830200" :question/math-academy-id "q-182574" :question/problem "$\\,$ Either apologize, or she won't forgive you.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Apologize.\n\n- $B:$ She won't forgive you.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't apologize.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."} :step/id #uuid "ec1c4e29-ed0c-5082-8077-e13084a4137b"}] :activity/title "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422157}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436634} :activity/id #uuid "fdb480fb-7595-51de-b205-79626369178f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805 :topic/id #uuid "155403ed-fbfa-59a9-b928-e762a73145ef" :topic/math-academy-id 247} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed0ada9a-ae18-56dc-945c-e726b9629412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R∧¬S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379169} {:answer/id #uuid "cad24836-75dd-516c-a038-882a63b33d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R∨S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379170} {:answer/id #uuid "4f308980-10ba-5049-a065-4747bca1db7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬R∧S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379171} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5eca90-785e-51eb-908c-bca925c96fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R∧S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379172} {:answer/id #uuid "0171c62e-9fde-5f60-8d20-351bf3c0fc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬R∨¬S" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379172} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac9cb155-d3eb-5181-abad-27d716b8bac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbe33c35-e591-5f89-871d-e169a25c3483" :question/math-academy-id "q-182498" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(R⇒¬S).$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬(R⇒¬S)≡¬(¬R∨¬S)\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬R∨¬S)≡¬(¬R)∧¬(¬S).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nR∧S.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $R∧S.$"} :step/id #uuid "8b066a8e-899d-55a1-8411-df8d5e53b232" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048fd58d-6161-542c-aca0-9aa3830ec64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Wear a jacket, and you won't get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401738} {:answer/id #uuid "2c28a06a-e16c-5c23-a019-7042d53b30bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401739} {:answer/id #uuid "1568255d-6e74-51c6-b213-0baccfa15504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you don’t wear a jacket, then you won't get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401740} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1a52bd-b4c3-59f6-aa7e-00e77fc5e146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you don’t wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401741} {:answer/id #uuid "7703df10-5599-5c0e-b2b9-a7aaaab873cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you wear a jacket, then you won't get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401741} :answer-field/id #uuid "970b9ff4-c62f-56de-9540-47a07451659c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98ecba05-28ef-5ea8-9d45-86a0357f6077" :question/math-academy-id "q-182592" :question/problem "$\\,$ Wear a jacket, or you will get cold.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Wear a jacket.\n\n- $B:$ You will get cold.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't wear a jacket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't wear a jacket, then you will get cold."} :step/id #uuid "b746c91b-d837-5aea-9df8-0783cb162ef2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba978700-923a-5354-858c-a21e380e4180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you don’t pay the bill, then your power won't be shut off." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057278} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a78960-7dba-518a-b058-7c5f72b56d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you don’t pay the bill, then your power will be shut off." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057279} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee08dff-f4b5-5a3f-a9c2-d1fdd107e7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you pay the bill, then your power won't be shut off." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057280} {:answer/id #uuid "4b852cf1-37a3-5032-9617-33b57eb2c017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "If you pay the bill, then your power will be shut off." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057281} {:answer/id #uuid "541fca0b-9881-50b2-8e11-90eb3ddc0a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Pay the bill, and your power won't be shut off." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186057279} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c7a8262-d702-5814-a615-315b3eeb2cc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "443b6ab8-c574-5d6b-85ea-335ba45a30ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-182591" :question/problem "$\\,$ Pay the bill, or your power will be shut off.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Pay the bill.\n\n- $B:$ Your power will be shut off.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't pay the bill.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't pay the bill, then your power will be shut off."} :step/id #uuid "c7860837-e731-5c21-a8c2-238981203349" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de943075-23ed-550c-9348-3beb6a4a1c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389679} {:answer/id #uuid "8b02c54e-2d06-5289-a555-a7919b41a546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389680} {:answer/id #uuid "b98c7d3c-5c1d-57c7-bf18-b70064ffde0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∨¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389681} {:answer/id #uuid "5c092ed6-6792-5c60-919a-67c8b2cefe42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389682} {:answer/id #uuid "3560a2b5-61fc-59ef-b588-2de0e12fe534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389680} :answer-field/id #uuid "566667e2-62c5-546d-bc15-bcb677d80fec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ab5fd68-e114-5907-8def-2141c3cce14d" :question/math-academy-id "q-171257" :question/problem "$X⇒Y\\,≡$" :question/worked-solution "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nX⇒Y\\,≡\\,¬X∨Y\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d47622de-c52c-55f3-b089-ee15881a00d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc52a1a-3940-5732-a868-a532984c3e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P∧¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436623} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd4cd09-8cbb-5ab2-9eee-c2ef187bad59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q∨P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436624} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcc4291-0a68-5242-920b-da5bf575f0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q∨¬P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436625} {:answer/id #uuid "baf71b07-e263-5232-88b3-0e6cfa44ae4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P∨¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436626} {:answer/id #uuid "ec239135-a191-53fd-ab45-832fc11ac4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "¬P∧¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436623} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b770ae3-a6bf-565c-b1f8-035f0cda7d32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1135e70-c8a0-50ee-ba8b-a60797a22c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-1477" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(P⇒Q).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(P⇒Q)≡¬(¬P∨Q).\n$$\n\nWe can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(¬P∨Q)≡¬(¬P)∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nFinally, canceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬P)∧¬Q≡P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $P∧¬Q.$"} :step/id #uuid "fa95311a-452f-53c8-b4cc-be5bc9963eec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436633}}] :activity/title "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436622}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120666} :activity/id #uuid "716a7135-4378-53ee-a90e-dd04ad76a6dc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814 :topic/id #uuid "15c8115d-e746-5dd5-ab8a-ad335bac2d26" :topic/math-academy-id 1849} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121032 :tutorial/content "The **real coordinate space** (of dimension $n$) is the set of all possible column vectors of height $n,$ whose components are real numbers. Symbolically, we represent the real coordinate space of dimension $n$ as $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the following vectors are in $\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^5,$ respectively: \n\n![](images/20/20e0adb04d7a8711cad4a9ee7ad26755d52176fc9549d7809fe10665a8118945.svg)\n\nWe've already encountered several operations on vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ or $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ namely, vector addition and subtraction, scalar multiplication, and the dot product. All of these operations work the same way in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "084d7eb8-e6bd-5678-98b5-149f18e61a8b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3182 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7383fe70-62a2-5560-94ff-c67e603fd1a9" :step/math-academy-id 17446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119612 :question/id #uuid "13fa2a8c-de7c-59ee-a838-2997494edc56" :question/math-academy-id "e-4782"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ce19559-0f08-5ef7-9ce4-58e8e14094ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049537}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411272}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Multiple of a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "5cfee280-6899-59d1-9ef8-d9880087735a" :step/math-academy-id 17447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119802 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119613 :question/id #uuid "1e82713d-4063-5bcd-bc2e-1d35c831ab34" :question/math-academy-id "e-4783"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab0d2c2f-e7a4-51bb-a20a-f28c2f7ded8b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411271}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Multiples of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "5aec48fe-0c24-5fa5-ac63-78e2260f7ead" :step/math-academy-id 17448 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121033 :tutorial/content "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n- The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n![](images/fc/fc78b2f4e9262108a217e57fec67e096b8b8b18e1486bec8e51df60b8afdfdfa.svg)\n\n- **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n![](images/e7/e78025f3481423365b2e924b6f8ba4e59f41e0c644ced7d2263238b219cba27a.svg)\n\n- The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n$$\n\\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a465d6d9-270d-5cd3-98c8-a78c527b3558" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3183 :tutorial/title "General Rules"} :step/id #uuid "21085223-43d0-5726-b75d-b102bd093330" :step/math-academy-id 17449 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119614 :question/id #uuid "ba38ff28-b4f3-59b8-b18a-a278db26294d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4784"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec386a91-fc52-56d8-8531-7e78a8464380" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411268}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dot Product of Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "4acc22bf-58df-50bc-b180-3077f975037c" :step/math-academy-id 17450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119615 :question/id #uuid "3ec5f9d5-327b-59e5-8df7-fb37d4054021" :question/math-academy-id "e-4785"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94f8e0e5-8d8a-516a-8f46-d76e94305df4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368723}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Quantity in an Expression Involving the Dot Product"} :step/id #uuid "36416450-4cfa-51cd-be65-4dead60ba52e" :step/math-academy-id 17451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121062 :tutorial/content "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n![](images/1f/1f1b79f2e61fccbb27944284cbdd217a311a7f795100cfbb56340a457814b407.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n![](images/6c/6c377966aa9fa1d581c0f9e3ba16bceb4020471914d5c4fb61118ff7cd709466.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "34cc8b84-ed7a-54e2-995c-12a2b57b0ff0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3922 :tutorial/title "Adding Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "6574b7ab-c36d-5af6-9074-943a1db9174c" :step/math-academy-id 23156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120668}}] :activity/title "Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120043}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120897} :activity/id #uuid "4a64e93c-7c38-5a2c-bcd0-c1efea32558e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308 :topic/id #uuid "3ec7e468-923a-5188-be34-311799abe7bd" :topic/math-academy-id 1806} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121051 :tutorial/content "Any plane can be uniquely determined by a point and two non-parallel direction vectors. For example, let $\\Pi$ be the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,2)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 0,3,0 \\rangle$  and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle -1,1,1 \\rangle.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea4ce822abec2b68d2471a2787f4e045493ca43c1c6795a7e0069de80cb5adee.png)\n\nUsing the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we can construct a **parametric equation** of the plane.\n\nFirst, let $R$ be any general point in the plane, with position vector $\\mathbf{r}.$ Notice that we can get from $P$ to $R$ using a linear combination of $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ with some (unknown for us) coefficients $s$ and $t$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b40f91c3d75f092c7b29f19770364e7518d37b64ba594a311a762ad79a950e4f.png)\n\nSo, we can write the **parametric equation** of the plane as\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{r}} = \\mathbf{p}+s{\\color{blue}\\,\\mathbf{b}}+t\\,{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{c}},\n\t\\qquad s,t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis formula works whenever $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are not parallel.\n\nIn our case, $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are indeed not parallel to each other. So, using column vector notation, the parametric equation for the plane $\\Pi$ is given by\n![](images/0d/0d40f8f87e1ed3f3bf5040e925e545169f5528f32cf8dd0052375aa560cc1381.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2828346e-3bca-51a8-a6ef-a86ca7034a62" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3257 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9e58d46a-ae91-5ee2-88e7-83dddd91cd81" :step/math-academy-id 421992 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119933 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119647 :question/id #uuid "a6fded96-287a-53ee-8b1c-dd618562b2c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4940"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02f02f1-f270-55e0-a7a9-0b729c4d82c0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410902}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "9f747cb6-7704-53df-bc68-9a201f538aa3" :step/math-academy-id 421993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119934 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119664 :question/id #uuid "b86eace4-01cb-5601-8e9b-2658e81fc656" :question/math-academy-id "e-5884"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62196ae9-df2e-5a3a-bc0a-1c0f4c40af3e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429817}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector To a Plane Given by Parametric Equation"} :step/id #uuid "aa340a23-a5f7-53c0-a279-cfaa2cb34f68" :step/math-academy-id 421996 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119935 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119648 :question/id #uuid "297bda8f-6e33-5cc2-a2e1-5ab6186fa9d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4941"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3061cb06-f57f-57c8-8821-dbd962b1e78e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398347}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Lines"} :step/id #uuid "0b7d4f28-8841-5518-ad36-74e1aa0d9163" :step/math-academy-id 421999 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121052 :tutorial/content "Any plane can be uniquely determined by three non-collinear points. \n\n![](images/08/08e65c4e3465a4bf155dfed6bc0dc2a35bf2eb9326d9bff0c0aa75b86e97d76b.png)\n\nTo write down a parametric equation of the plane, the trick is to use the three non-collinear points to construct two displacement vectors that are parallel to the plane.\n\n![](images/16/1613ea9df76b637b93e0e4b38b70b87892140398eaa7aaccb74f2a5642124dfd.png)\n\nThen we can proceed as usual to find the equation of the plane. Let's practice this technique in the following example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e32f2dc4-38f6-541d-9dc6-9cfd1ac079eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3258 :tutorial/title "Finding the Equation of a Plane Passing Through Three Points"} :step/id #uuid "43769a60-5b42-5a2c-82fa-0054a159b437" :step/math-academy-id 422002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119649 :question/id #uuid "9c36ddd5-fcf1-5c4d-a350-aa8ef3a3ca9e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4942"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d11f13f0-1cfa-59ec-a04a-9483d942b1aa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429941}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Passing Through Three Given Points"} :step/id #uuid "a2d3f79a-dc28-5d90-9d1f-404334ec9529" :step/math-academy-id 422003}] :activity/title "The Parametric Equations of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120017}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423364} :activity/id #uuid "3089dbbe-cc2f-5f60-8b4c-786b3272c8cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308 :topic/id #uuid "3ec7e468-923a-5188-be34-311799abe7bd" :topic/math-academy-id 1806} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd010f61-f497-566f-ac0f-c50c695f2870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390876} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fe09e5-ac83-5e62-97ff-7cc91f6b67c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,6,2⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩+t⟨6,-5,10⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390877} {:answer/id #uuid "5b991d71-53b1-55cf-8e90-ebe111f71f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨8,1,0⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390878} {:answer/id #uuid "c86fee90-6ade-5c0b-9714-4278894c742b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨8,1,0⟩+t⟨6,-5,10⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390879} {:answer/id #uuid "db01346f-8d56-5827-aa82-3725170f51a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390880} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf886e4d-3534-5b42-92f4-88d2e9d1dbe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0602f3f6-acc2-53ef-a72e-b71caa5d5289" :question/math-academy-id "q-33066" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $A(5,-3,1)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨8,1,0⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩$ and $r=⟨6,-5,10⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨3,2,5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,6,2⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $A(5,-3,1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $a=⟨5,-3,1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f9f2ed5-353f-52bf-a2a3-853d4a9582d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423365}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027279ba-c86b-5b53-9b4f-d91a6c95b4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336052} {:answer/id #uuid "2b335037-3eda-5eb1-a665-40e27aacca2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-5,3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336439} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc9ad4f-0956-53af-9375-a4dc1247e499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-5,3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336605} {:answer/id #uuid "924674c9-4ac4-5fac-bce9-febfcdbeeeee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨7,-3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337359} {:answer/id #uuid "faf5ff12-8cff-5606-9806-8c51dfe352b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336439} :answer-field/id #uuid "0006422d-58d9-51c7-8d8f-f4fd7450f036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2e1c378-a183-516b-92fd-1753dcbc375f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70972" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨0,-1,3⟩$ and $w=⟨1,2,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-5i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨-5,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-5,3,1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "43654397-35b1-5e26-a4f3-f83c5d045414" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423366}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423366 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7586c1e4-84f3-5128-898b-c5e464276c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨6,2,1⟩+s⟨4,-1,5⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337090} {:answer/id #uuid "82d1d2de-9e64-5f24-9d44-872a4f4b17ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨4,-1,5⟩+s⟨6,2,1⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337221} {:answer/id #uuid "85ed694c-09f9-5528-8f94-aa3dcea698e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,2,3⟩+s⟨4,-1,5⟩+t⟨6,2,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337243} {:answer/id #uuid "9294f976-ff7c-58c2-93a2-681c99675cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337366} {:answer/id #uuid "d0355145-d653-5b7e-b879-a79de4840c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨6,2,1⟩+s⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,9⟩+t⟨3,10,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337090} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6f8717-8af1-5d47-bcaf-6f6357145f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63af645f-746e-5e92-b7a0-0217edd376c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-70977" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $D(6,2,1)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,9⟩+s⟨4,-1,5⟩$ and $r=⟨\\sqrt{3},10,-1⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨4,-1,5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $D(6,2,1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $d=⟨6,2,1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =d+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨6,2,1⟩+s⟨4,-1,5⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f22fda97-7410-513e-bdd3-694b01fc4928"}] :activity/title "The Parametric Equations of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423363}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429946} :activity/id #uuid "f1f3e620-4475-5a3a-a265-6ab29906aca2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308 :topic/id #uuid "3ec7e468-923a-5188-be34-311799abe7bd" :topic/math-academy-id 1806} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840901d2-388a-56b4-b2ed-4aa835792fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨2,1,-\\frac{1}{5}⟩+s⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨-3,2,7⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393059} {:answer/id #uuid "3a999e63-69bc-5b5b-ba4a-b3d5b35bdaf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,2,7⟩+s⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨2,1,-\\frac{1}{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393060} {:answer/id #uuid "a9567360-7eeb-5790-a68a-54789a38c8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393061} {:answer/id #uuid "0abc7499-ef02-5049-b4d5-92446ade1dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,2,7⟩+s⟨3,-1,20⟩+t⟨0,3,-4⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393062} {:answer/id #uuid "d56ba868-cc5e-5eb8-8190-756b44636c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,0,1⟩+s⟨-3,2,7⟩+t⟨2,1,-\\frac{1}{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393060} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bd7e12-82f3-5b83-88f9-1acb2c12ad74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1e3ae2d-0bf0-5679-8209-36898e366f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-70981" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $H(-3,2,7)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨3,-1,20⟩+s⟨-3,0,1⟩$ and $r=⟨0,3,-4⟩+t⟨2,1,-\\frac{1}{5}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨2,1,-\\frac{1}{5}⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $H(-3,2,7)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $h=⟨-3,2,7⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =h+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨-3,2,7⟩+s⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨2,1,-\\frac{1}{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6962e54d-afff-5346-b8ad-2f0747169762" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076c0841-49e0-5efa-932b-cea12451f354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,-2,5⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429935} {:answer/id #uuid "36f17219-1319-57b9-b736-3daca3fe9f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,-2,5⟩+t⟨-2,4,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429936} {:answer/id #uuid "42182090-ca90-5aff-919b-f97f28d17f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429937} {:answer/id #uuid "a639639c-9709-5ea4-b9d7-eed8e4f97ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429938} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4f3967-0094-5670-b34b-c4d617495978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,4,3⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429937} :answer-field/id #uuid "918bca49-2085-5b3c-9bbd-1ed60948277f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e59ecbbf-3ac2-51fb-8da8-83763d787a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71033" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $D(1,0,-2),\\,E(-2,-2,5)$, and $F(-2,4,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨-2,-2,5⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨-2,4,3⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $d=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =d+s\\overset{\\to }{DE}+t\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\ r & =⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80c6a6f7-a534-55d9-a6a9-83c73859670a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429944}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4a3c41-2d3e-5406-a2cd-cbab49cb529e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨6,-4,-26⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260257} {:answer/id #uuid "63d710f5-99cb-53b4-8290-6299023ee722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨8,-2,17⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260737} {:answer/id #uuid "95c11743-7580-55cc-a7f1-587f795b6ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-8,-2,-22⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261175} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1ed729-7d24-5fef-a919-3b87d20a7b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-8,2,-11⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261244} {:answer/id #uuid "da606c57-af3e-52fa-9552-99fdba561ef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,-8,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261175} :answer-field/id #uuid "e859b94c-82b3-57fc-b159-f1a0791b5216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58d7da64-39e5-5263-82e1-d46d43bc9c9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-128497" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=⟨3,\\sqrt{7},-2⟩+t⟨-2,8,0⟩+s⟨3,-1,-1⟩,\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-2,8,0⟩$ and $w=⟨3,-1,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i-2j-22k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "87cee5fb-f33c-5202-a9b9-1f5b449bede4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259294bf-42a6-5b1f-a914-2eeba4786e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,12,-7⟩+s⟨1,3,-5⟩+t⟨1,6,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260203} {:answer/id #uuid "3660d937-cc87-5a73-a31f-13375df8d401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-7,12,-3⟩+s⟨-5,3,1⟩+t⟨1,5,6⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260360} {:answer/id #uuid "586437d1-d3c1-5c04-82a5-9e97897d0755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-7,-3,12⟩+s⟨-3,4,2⟩+t⟨1,5,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260630} {:answer/id #uuid "5a80a400-edf2-5ed4-95ef-bc093dec6a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-7,-3,12⟩+s⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨1,5,7⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260648} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a3a0fb-37c8-5001-bfec-42aa7883b3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-7,12,-3⟩+s⟨-3,5,1⟩+t⟨1,6,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260360} :answer-field/id #uuid "991622fb-4bb5-597a-a633-df7720b54e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75465043-c7d7-54b7-b914-8b78a961bb97" :question/math-academy-id "q-128536" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $B(-7,12,-3)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨-7,2,4⟩+s⟨-5,3,1⟩$ and $r=⟨-22,17,13⟩+t⟨1,5,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-5,3,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,5,6⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $B(-7,12,-3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $b=⟨-7,12,-3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =b+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨-7,12,-3⟩+s⟨-5,3,1⟩+t⟨1,5,6⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a96d0f38-f22e-54d6-9800-3b945209e167"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e02ac0-3e89-5c73-aee7-9f2de3148d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391684} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffc566d-8d4c-53c3-af4b-b2d7a4d35946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391685} {:answer/id #uuid "efc39529-4f0f-5e00-b796-0c632007c03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391686} {:answer/id #uuid "f83846d5-42d7-540e-ad5c-05350b13e9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391687} {:answer/id #uuid "0693f405-c3d1-5022-a785-4b3d44119374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391684} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aebbae6-4b97-5e3f-9a17-15682100023d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3b07ac22-3309-5bc0-ac4b-6e1be0e5a45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-33823" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=⟨1,1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,2,2⟩,\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-1,1,1⟩$ and $w=⟨0,2,2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,2,-2⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "b87c320e-e438-5e5e-b4bc-c722b9da8264" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429942}}] :activity/title "The Parametric Equations of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429934}]}
